cma.resource/--background--                       cma.resource/--background--

   CMA
	cma.resource manages one zone of uncached, physically contiguous
	memory that is set aside for the use of DMA engines and of the
	VideoCore. The zone is reserved by the Emu68 firmware (the "cma"
	device tree overlay, for example dtoverlay=cma,size=8 in user.txt,
	size in megabytes) and announced as the property reg = <base size> of
	the node /emu68/cma@0 of the device tree. When the node is absent or
	empty the resource does not load.

	The zone is not part of the memory lists of exec.library: AllocMem()
	never hands it out. Programs take memory from it with the calls of
	this resource. A block is contiguous, lies inside the zone and is
	never cached by the CPU, so the data written by a program or by a DMA
	engine is seen by the other side without cache maintenance.

	Open the resource with OpenResource("cma.resource"), a resource has no
	OpenLibrary() and no CloseLibrary(), and fail cleanly when it is not
	there: it exists only on a system that was configured with the
	overlay.

	The allocator is TLSF. Sizes are rounded up to a multiple of 16 bytes,
	and every block costs a header taken from the zone, so CMA_Avail()
	starts a little under the size of the zone (64 bytes under it on the
	first version) and a request of almost the whole free size is refused.
	The calls take a semaphore: do not use them from an interrupt or under
	Forbid()/Disable().

	Reading the zone is slower than reading cached memory (measured on a
	PiStorm: about 11 times slower than FAST RAM), writing is not. Keep in
	it what the hardware needs to share, and copy the rest to normal
	memory.

   VERSIONS
	The number is the version of the resource: lib_Version.lib_Revision. A
	call that was added after the first version has its version at the end
	of its NAME line, like "(V1.5)". A change in what a call does is
	written in its FUNCTION text, "As of V1.5 ..." and "Prior to V1.5
	...". A call without a version in its NAME has been there since 1.0.

	1.0 20.10.2026 First version: CMA_AllocMem(), CMA_AllocAligned(),
	                 CMA_ReallocMem(), CMA_ReallocAligned(), CMA_FreeMem(),
	                 and CMA_Avail().

cma.resource/CMA_AllocAligned                   cma.resource/CMA_AllocAligned

   NAME
	CMA_AllocAligned -- allocate an aligned block of uncached memory

   SYNOPSIS
	aligned = CMA_AllocAligned( byte_size, alignment )
	d0                          d0         d1

	APTR CMA_AllocAligned(ULONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Like CMA_AllocMem(), but the address of the block is a multiple of
	alignment. Use it for what a DMA engine or the VideoCore wants at a
	given boundary, such as 16, 64 or 4096 bytes.

   INPUTS
	byte_size       size of the block in bytes
	alignment       the block starts at a multiple of this many bytes: a
	                power of two

   RESULTS
	aligned         the block, or NULL when the zone has no free block
	                that large

   NOTE
	An alignment above 2 MB is taken as 2 MB, and a value that is not a
	power of two is rounded up to one. The call takes the semaphore of
	the resource: do not use it from an interrupt or with
	Forbid()/Disable() in force. Release the block with CMA_FreeMem().

   SEE ALSO
	cma.resource/CMA_AllocMem(),
	cma.resource/CMA_FreeMem()

cma.resource/CMA_AllocMem                           cma.resource/CMA_AllocMem

   NAME
	CMA_AllocMem -- allocate a block of uncached contiguous memory

   SYNOPSIS
	mem = CMA_AllocMem( byte_size )
	d0                  d0

	APTR CMA_AllocMem(ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Takes a block of byte_size bytes from the zone. The size is rounded up
	to a multiple of 16 bytes. The block lies entirely in the zone, in one
	piece, and its contents are not defined: the call does not clear it.

   INPUTS
	byte_size       size of the block in bytes

   RESULTS
	mem             the block, or NULL when the zone has no free block
	                that large, or when byte_size is 0

   NOTE
	The call takes the semaphore of the resource: it can wait, so do not
	use it from an interrupt or with Forbid()/Disable() in force. Release
	the block with CMA_FreeMem().

   SEE ALSO
	cma.resource/CMA_AllocAligned(),
	cma.resource/CMA_FreeMem(),
	cma.resource/CMA_Avail()

cma.resource/CMA_Avail                                 cma.resource/CMA_Avail

   NAME
	CMA_Avail -- free memory left in the zone

   SYNOPSIS
	bytes = CMA_Avail()
	d0

	ULONG CMA_Avail(void);

   FUNCTION
	Returns how many bytes of the zone are free, the sum of all free
	blocks.

   RESULTS
	bytes           free bytes in the zone

   NOTE
	The free memory may be split into several blocks: the largest block
	that can be allocated is smaller than this value, and a request for
	almost all of it is refused. A new zone reports 64 bytes less than its
	size.

   SEE ALSO
	cma.resource/CMA_AllocMem()

cma.resource/CMA_FreeMem                             cma.resource/CMA_FreeMem

   NAME
	CMA_FreeMem -- return a block to the zone

   SYNOPSIS
	CMA_FreeMem( buffer )
	             a0

	VOID CMA_FreeMem(APTR);

   FUNCTION
	Gives the block back to the zone. The size is known to the allocator,
	it is not passed. Free neighbours are joined, so a zone that was used
	up is whole again once everything has been freed.

   INPUTS
	buffer          a block from CMA_AllocMem(), CMA_AllocAligned() or
	                CMA_ReallocMem() or CMA_ReallocAligned

   NOTE
	Only pass what one of the allocation calls of this resource returned,
	never memory of exec.library, and free a block once. The call takes
	the semaphore of the resource: do not use it from an interrupt or with
	Forbid()/Disable() in force.

   SEE ALSO
	cma.resource/CMA_AllocMem(),
	cma.resource/CMA_AllocAligned(),
	cma.resource/CMA_ReallocMem(),
	cma.resource/CMA_ReallocAligned()

cma.resource/CMA_ReallocAligned               cma.resource/CMA_ReallocAligned

   NAME
	CMA_ReallocAligned -- change the size of a block

   SYNOPSIS
	pointer = CMA_ReallocAligned( buffer, new_size, alignment )
	d0                            a0      d0        d1

	APTR CMA_ReallocAligned(APTR, ULONG, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Grows or shrinks buffer to new_size bytes while keeping the requested
	alignment. The data is kept up to the smaller of the old and the new 
	size. The block may move: use the returned pointer from now on and 
	not buffer. The alignment argument is used only of the block is moved.

   INPUTS
	buffer          a block from CMA_AllocMem(), CMA_AllocAligned() or
	                CMA_ReallocMem()
	new_size        the size wanted, in bytes
	alignment       new alignment, used if block is moved

   RESULTS
	pointer         the block, at the same address or at a new one, or
	                NULL when it could not be resized

   NOTE
	An alignment above 2 MB is taken as 2 MB, and a value that is not a
	power of two is rounded up to one. The alignment argument is used only 
	in case of block movement and ignored if the block is resized in place.
	The call takes the semaphore of the resource: do not use it from an 
	interrupt or with Forbid()/Disable() in force.

   SEE ALSO
	cma.resource/CMA_AllocMem(),
	cma.resource/CMA_FreeMem(),
	cma.resource/CMA_ReallocMem()

cma.resource/CMA_ReallocMem                       cma.resource/CMA_ReallocMem

   NAME
	CMA_ReallocMem -- change the size of a block

   SYNOPSIS
	pointer = CMA_ReallocMem( buffer, new_size )
	d0                        a0      d0

	APTR CMA_ReallocMem(APTR, ULONG);

   FUNCTION
	Grows or shrinks buffer to new_size bytes. The data is kept up to the
	smaller of the old and the new size. The block may move: use the
	returned pointer from now on and not buffer.

   INPUTS
	buffer          a block from CMA_AllocMem(), CMA_AllocAligned() or
	                CMA_ReallocMem()
	new_size        the size wanted, in bytes

   RESULTS
	pointer         the block, at the same address or at a new one, or
	                NULL when it could not be resized

   NOTE
	Do not assume that the block keeps the alignment it was allocated with
	when it moves. The call takes the semaphore of the resource: do not
	use it from an interrupt or with Forbid()/Disable() in force.

   SEE ALSO
	cma.resource/CMA_AllocMem(),
	cma.resource/CMA_FreeMem()

